    "available_at": "2026-10-19T16:48:02.881568+00:00",
    "body": "Hitachi Energy will invest more than $330 million in a new transformer insulation factory in London, Ontario. The project will bring production of a key material used inside transformers to Canada for the first time.\nWhat’s Related\nThe 226,000-square-foot facility is expected to be completed by 2028 and create more than 100 jobs . It will manufacture electrical insulation pressboard and laminates for transformer production.\nThe factory will establish Canada’s first pressboard manufacturing line and Hitachi Energy’s first in the Americas. Planned annual output is up to 20,000 metric tons, with materials going to the company’s transformer operations in Varennes, Quebec, and other manufacturers in North America and overseas.\nFederal and provincial governments are contributing up to approximately $35.3 million toward the project. That includes about $21.2 million from the Canadian government and up to $14.1 million from Ontario.\nWhat is pressboard?\nPressboard is an insulation material made from wood fiber. Inside transformers, it provides electrical insulation and helps with cooling, so its quality and consistency affect how well the equipment works and how long it lasts.\nThe London facility will use four machines called formers, which shape wet pulp into sheets. Ontario says it will be the world's first transformer pressboard factory with four of these machines.\nLocated in London’s Huron Industrial Park, the plant will use manufacturing technology and automation to turn wood fiber into finished insulation materials. The federal government says the project will source key inputs locally, creating business for Canadian suppliers, including pulp producers.\n“This investment reflects our long-term commitment to Canadian industry, talent and innovation in an electric economy ,” said Carla Vicente, country managing director of Hitachi Energy Canada.\nLoosening up a bottleneck\nHitachi Energy says limited supplies of specialized insulation materials remain a bottleneck for transformer manufacturers. The new plant is intended to bring production closer to North American customers as electricity demand grows.\nThe announcement identifies industrial projects, AI and data centers, expanding communities, and electrification as sources of that demand. The factory will supply materials needed to build the transformers that serve those projects.\n“This project demonstrates that there are high-value opportunities for forest products in critical energy infrastructure ,” said Tim Hodgson, Canada’s Minister of Energy and Natural Resources.\nBuilding on investments in Ontario and Quebec\nThe project follows Hitachi Energy’s June announcement of an agreement to acquire Canduct Group, an Ontario-based manufacturer of transformer insulation kits and components. Canduct serves equipment manufacturers and repair companies in the United States and Canada and has supplied Hitachi Energy for more than 20 years.\nHitachi Energy also announced a $195 million expansion in Varennes in September 2025. That project is intended to nearly triple the site’s large power transformer production, while the new London factory will supply insulation materials to those operations.",
